From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: h713-hdmirx: leave out a block whose page the firmware's allocator has reached, and two comments corrected

The driver keeps its blocks at fixed offsets in the cpu_comm shared memory:
the HDCP 2.2 key at +0x36000, the Wce_SetWindow structures at +0x37000, the
backlight PWM words at +0x37100 (only with allwinner,backlight-pwm-info) and,
far above, the staging area of THal_Vp_Init at +0x400000. Below them the
display firmware's allocator (Trid_SMM) grows upwards and does not know that
the places exist. Its header lies at +0x2d000 (word 47 the heap's start, word
45 the break as an offset from it); the break is always page aligned, because
a large block takes whole pages and a class of small blocks takes a page of
its own, so the smallest thing the firmware ever gives out is a page. On a
HY310 (18.10.2026, after a boot, a standby and a session of colour work) it
has given out one page: the break stands at +0x33000, three pages below the
key. Nothing in our Linux calls the allocator, so nothing moves it, but
nothing would have said so either if it had: the driver wrote its blocks
without looking, and a block written into a page the firmware has taken is
a crashed display firmware or a key overwritten, with no line in the log.

The driver now reads the break before it writes a block. All three blocks are
written in the init sequence, once per bind, so that is where the reads are,
one before each: if the break stands at or above the first byte of the
block's page, the page is the allocator's next block or already given out,
and the step is left out with a warning that names the step, the break and
the place. Nothing is written into the page and the call that would have read
it is not made. The way out is one the driver already has: the key step is
left out as if there were no key file (the hdcp attribute says "skipped" and
why), the PWM words as on a board without the property, and the window call
is not made, which leaves the firmware with the window it booted with (not
measured). Warning only was the
other choice. It was not taken because a block in a page the allocator owns
can corrupt the firmware's heap, which is a worse and harder to find failure
than a missing HDCP 2.2 key, and because it is the rule h713-tv already
follows for its own blocks in the same window. The places themselves are not
moved: the firmware may keep the pointers it was given, and h713-tv's blocks
sit behind these. The test is against the page, not the byte, for the reason
the break is page aligned: a break standing exactly at the start of the
block's page means the very next allocation takes it.

While the break is below the places nothing changes except one info line at
bind, which says where the break is and where the lowest place is. If the
allocator's header is not where the cpu_comm driver puts it, nothing is known
and the driver does what it did, with one warning. THal_Vp_Init's staging
area is not checked: the driver writes nothing into it, and it lies 4 MiB up.

Two comments in the same driver are corrected, both found wrong when the
white balance was brought up (the messages of 0094 and 0101 keep the old
wording; the tree has the right one). The table's note on SetWhiteBalance
said the call takes a pointer to three factory-calibrated doubles that are
not available. It takes one word, the physical address of a block of six
32-bit words, {R, G, B gain, R, G, B offset + 512}, each 0..1023, and six
times 512 is neutral, which is also what the registers hold with the stock
firmware: no factory data is missing. The note on the controls that are
absent said gamma and white balance were already GAMMA_LUT and CTM on the
display CRTC. For gamma that is right (the vendor's HAL computes its curves
on the ARM and writes the same stage). White balance is not: CTM only folds a
gain into the gamma LUT, while the firmware has a gain and an offset per
channel of its own, which the stock firmware uses and h713-tv now sets.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c  |  139 ++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 127 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -564,6 +564,23 @@ static const phys_addr_t h713_hdmirx_slot_c[H713_HDMIRX_SLOTS] = {
 #define H713_MAP_WCE		(H713_SHM_WCE - H713_SHM_HDCP22)
 #define H713_MAP_BLPWM		(H713_SHM_BLPWM - H713_SHM_HDCP22)
 
+/*
+ * The firmware's allocator (Trid_SMM) owns the rest of the shared memory, the
+ * places above included, and it does not know of them. Its header lies at
+ * SHMEM_OFF_SMM_HEAP (0x2ccf0) rounded up to a page; the page table follows,
+ * then the data, which grows upwards a page at a time (the break is always
+ * page aligned, and a class of small blocks takes a page of its own). Word 47
+ * of the header is the heap's start, a physical address; word 45 is the break
+ * as an offset from it. A page the break has reached may be given out by the
+ * firmware at any moment, or already is. Measured on the HY310 (18.10.2026):
+ * one page given, the break at +0x33000, the lowest place three pages above.
+ * h713-tv makes the same test before it writes its own blocks in this window.
+ */
+#define H713_SMM_HEAD		0x0002d000
+#define H713_SMM_BRK_WORD	45
+#define H713_SMM_START_WORD	47
+#define H713_SMM_PAGE		0x1000
+
 #define H713_HDCP22_SIZE	912
 #define H713_FW_HDCP22		"hy310-hdcp22.bin"
 
@@ -678,10 +695,12 @@ struct h713_hdmirx_step {
  * session numbers below) by way of analyse/hdmi-seq/hdmi_seq.py, which has run
  * this sequence on the device.
  *
- * Stock session 16, SetWhiteBalance, is missing on purpose: it takes a pointer
- * to three factory-calibrated doubles that this project does not have. The
- * userspace tool skips it for the same reason and the sequence works without
- * it.
+ * Stock session 16, SetWhiteBalance, is not in the table. Its one argument is
+ * the physical address of a block of six 32-bit words, {R, G, B gain, R, G, B
+ * offset + 512}, each 0..1023, and six times 512 is neutral -- the state the
+ * white balance registers hold with the stock firmware as well. No factory
+ * data is missing, and the sequence works without the call; the white balance
+ * is set from userspace, by h713-tv.
  */
 static const struct h713_hdmirx_step h713_hdmirx_init_seq[] = {
 	/* ---- phase 2: init and callback wiring ---------------------- */
@@ -852,14 +871,23 @@ static const struct h713_hdmirx_step h713_hdmirx_init_seq[] = {
  *
  * Deliberately absent, each for its own reason:
  *
- *  - Gamma and white balance. Both exist twice on this chip, and the half
- *    that is known to work is not this one: the DE2 gamma stage is offered
- *    as GAMMA_LUT and CTM on the display CRTC (patch 0095) and is measured
- *    to invert the picture on the console and on the video path alike
- *    (doku/87-gamma-ctm-kms.md section 0), while the firmware announces
- *    "Can not get MP GAMMAModuleID" for its own gamma module at every
- *    start. A V4L2_CID_GAMMA here would be a second owner of one visible
- *    quantity with no defined precedence between them.
+ *  - Gamma. It exists twice on this chip, and the half that is known to
+ *    work is not this one: the DE2 gamma stage is offered as GAMMA_LUT on the
+ *    display CRTC (patch 0095; the vendor's HAL computes its curves on the
+ *    ARM and writes this same stage) and is measured to invert the picture
+ *    on the console and on the video path alike (doku/87-gamma-ctm-kms.md
+ *    section 0), while the firmware announces "Can not get MP GAMMAModuleID"
+ *    for its own gamma module at every start. A V4L2_CID_GAMMA here would be
+ *    a second owner of one visible quantity with no defined precedence
+ *    between them.
+ *
+ *  - White balance. The CTM of the display CRTC does not stand in for it:
+ *    CTM can only fold a gain into the gamma LUT, while the firmware has a
+ *    white balance of its own, a gain and an offset for each of R, G and B
+ *    in registers it writes itself, and the stock firmware uses it
+ *    (THal_Vp_SetWhiteBalance). It is no V4L2 control because what matters
+ *    is a step on the base of the picture mode, which userspace keeps;
+ *    h713-tv sends the call.
  *
  *  - The backlight. The lamp already has a driver, pwm-backlight on
  *    PWM2/PB4, and a class device to go with it. Whether
@@ -1269,6 +1297,7 @@ struct h713_hdmirx_plane {
 enum h713_hdcp22_key_state {
 	H713_HDCP22_KEY_NOT_RUN,	/* the sequence has not reached the step */
 	H713_HDCP22_KEY_SKIPPED,	/* no key file: the step was left out */
+	H713_HDCP22_KEY_NO_PLACE,	/* the firmware's allocator had reached the key's page: left out */
 	H713_HDCP22_KEY_SENT,		/* THal_Vp_SetHDCP22Key accepted the key */
 };
 
@@ -1442,6 +1471,73 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_call(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, const char *name,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The break of the firmware's allocator as a physical address (see
+ * H713_SMM_HEAD). The page with the header is mapped for the two reads only:
+ * this driver's own window starts nine pages higher, and the header is the
+ * firmware's, not a place of ours. Uncached, like cpu_comm's own mapping.
+ */
+static int h713_hdmirx_smm_break(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u64 *brk)
+{
+	void __iomem *head;
+	u32 start, size;
+
+	head = ioremap(rx->shm_base + H713_SMM_HEAD, H713_SMM_PAGE);
+	if (!head)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	start = readl(head + 4 * H713_SMM_START_WORD);
+	size = readl(head + 4 * H713_SMM_BRK_WORD);
+	iounmap(head);
+	if (start != rx->shm_base + H713_SMM_HEAD)
+		return -ENXIO;
+	*brk = (u64)start + size;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * May this step write its block at the place? What counts is the page of the
+ * place, not its first byte: the break only moves in whole pages, so once it
+ * stands at the start of that page the allocator's next block is that page,
+ * and above it the page is the firmware's already. In both cases nothing is
+ * written, and the step is left out: the firmware reads the block through the
+ * address it is handed, and a block written into a page it may have taken for
+ * something else can break the firmware's heap; a step that is not sent only
+ * costs what the step does (the same way out as a missing key file or a
+ * missing PWM property). The driver cannot move a place, the firmware may keep
+ * the pointers and h713-tv's blocks sit behind them. Checked at every step
+ * that writes, because the break moves while the firmware runs. Where the
+ * allocator cannot be found the answer is yes, as it always was: nothing is
+ * known then, and the driver does what it did.
+ *
+ * THal_Vp_Init's staging area at H713_SHM_VP_INIT is not checked: the driver
+ * writes nothing there (the firmware copies its own data into it), and it lies
+ * 4 MiB up, behind a heap that would have to give out three quarters of the
+ * shared memory first.
+ */
+static bool h713_hdmirx_place_free(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 place,
+				   unsigned int step, const char *name)
+{
+	u64 brk;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = h713_hdmirx_smm_break(rx, &brk);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn_once(rx->dev,
+			      "the firmware's allocator cannot be read (%d): its break is not checked against the places of this driver\n",
+			      ret);
+		return true;
+	}
+	if (brk < rx->shm_base + ALIGN_DOWN(place, H713_SMM_PAGE))
+		return true;
+
+	dev_warn(rx->dev,
+		 "step %u %s skipped: the firmware's allocator has reached the page of its block (break +%#llx, block +%#x), nothing is written there\n",
+		 step, name, brk - rx->shm_base, place);
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Write the two window rectangles and the aspect selector into shared memory
  * and hand back their addresses. The shared memory is mapped write combining;
@@ -1504,8 +1600,14 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_load_hdcp_key(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 *args)
 static int h713_hdmirx_run_init_seq(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
 	unsigned int i;
+	u64 brk;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (!h713_hdmirx_smm_break(rx, &brk))
+		dev_info(rx->dev,
+			 "the firmware's allocator ends at +%#llx of the shared memory, the lowest place of this driver is at +%#x\n",
+			 brk - rx->shm_base, H713_SHM_HDCP22);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(h713_hdmirx_init_seq); i++) {
 		const struct h713_hdmirx_step *s = &h713_hdmirx_init_seq[i];
 		u32 args[6] = { 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
@@ -1529,6 +1631,8 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_run_init_seq(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 			dev_info(rx->dev,
 				 "backlight PWM: channel %u polarity %u freq %u vs_lock %u min %u max %u\n",
 				 pwm[0], pwm[1], pwm[2], pwm[3], pwm[4], pwm[5]);
+			if (!h713_hdmirx_place_free(rx, H713_SHM_BLPWM, i, s->name))
+				continue;
 			/* one argument: the address of the six words (see H713_SHM_BLPWM) */
 			for (w = 0; w < ARRAY_SIZE(pwm); w++)
 				writel(pwm[w], rx->shm + H713_MAP_BLPWM + w * 4);
@@ -1539,9 +1643,15 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_run_init_seq(struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 			args[2] = rx->shm_base + H713_SHM_VP_INIT;
 			break;
 		case H713_STEP_WCE:
+			if (!h713_hdmirx_place_free(rx, H713_SHM_WCE, i, s->name))
+				continue;
 			h713_hdmirx_wce_args(rx, args);
 			break;
 		case H713_STEP_HDCP:
+			if (!h713_hdmirx_place_free(rx, H713_SHM_HDCP22, i, s->name)) {
+				rx->hdcp22_key = H713_HDCP22_KEY_NO_PLACE;
+				continue;
+			}
 			ret = h713_hdmirx_load_hdcp_key(rx, args);
 			if (ret) {
 				dev_warn(rx->dev,
@@ -4978,6 +5088,8 @@ static const char *h713_hdmirx_hdcp_key22(const struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 		return "handed over";
 	case H713_HDCP22_KEY_SKIPPED:
 		return "skipped";
+	case H713_HDCP22_KEY_NO_PLACE:
+		return "skipped (the firmware's allocator had reached the place)";
 	default:
 		return "not yet";
 	}
@@ -5088,6 +5200,9 @@ static ssize_t hdcp_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
 		at += sysfs_emit_at(buf, at, "key-2.2:       skipped at probe (%s not loadable, %d): no 2.2 this boot\n",
 				    H713_FW_HDCP22, rx->hdcp22_key_err);
 		break;
+	case H713_HDCP22_KEY_NO_PLACE:
+		at += sysfs_emit_at(buf, at, "key-2.2:       skipped at probe (the firmware's allocator had reached the page of the key's place): no 2.2 this boot\n");
+		break;
 	default:
 		at += sysfs_emit_at(buf, at, "key-2.2:       the init sequence has not reached the step\n");
 		break;
